### Harsh Critic
I'll start by reading the paper end-to-end before writing the review.---

## Review: Nonparametric Covariance Regression for Massive Neural Data on Restricted Covariates via Graph

---

## Critical Issues

### 1. Title/scope mismatch: "massive neural data" is not demonstrated — **Structural**

The title and abstract repeatedly invoke "massive neural data," yet the simulations use *n* = 50 neurons, the LFP application uses *n* = 14 neurons, and the HC application uses *n* = 36 neurons. These are not massive by any neuroscience standard; modern silicon probes routinely record hundreds to thousands of units simultaneously. The computational profile confirms the limitation: inference runs on a laptop at 3.3–3.5 seconds per MCMC iteration. The Discussion section explicitly lists scalability as future work (variational inference, linear-time GP approximations). This is not a gap to fill — the current method is simply not a method for massive data. The framing is structurally misleading because the dimensionality reduction that motivates the factor model (*k* ≪ *n*) is never tested at the scale it is designed for. The central claim of the title cannot be supported by the experiments.

### 2. Unfair and uninterpretable dCMP baseline — **Structural**

Section 4.2 compares the latent factor models against dCMP (Wei and Stevenson, 2023), where dCMP is fit *separately per neuron*. This conflates two independent advantages of the proposed method: (a) modeling inter-neuron correlations jointly (which dCMP by design cannot do) and (b) having a more flexible observation model. The held-out log-likelihood gap (−9.90 × 10³ for dCMP vs. −5.89 × 10³ for L-GLGP-adaptive) could reflect either or both advantages. A fair comparison would include either (i) a joint CMP-based latent factor model, or (ii) a Poisson-log-normal latent factor model that assumes homoscedastic residuals across conditions. Without this, the experiment cannot isolate whether the *covariance regression* component—the paper's primary claimed contribution—contributes any value at all in real data. The comparison is not just incomplete; it is uninterpretable for the paper's stated purpose.

### 3. No comparison against a mean-only model — **Evidential**

The paper proposes *joint mean-covariance regression* as its core contribution, claiming that modeling covariate-dependent covariance provides scientific insight beyond the mean. However, in neither the simulation study nor the real data applications is there a comparison against a baseline that models the mean but assumes homoscedastic (or stationary) residual covariance. All comparisons (L-GP, L-GLGP-fixed, L-GLGP-adaptive) differ only in their kernel, not in whether covariance is modeled at all. The GPWP comparison in simulations is the closest to testing covariance regression vs. an alternative approach, but it tests a different covariance model rather than no covariance model. There is consequently no direct evidence in the paper that the covariance regression component — as opposed to the factor model for dimension reduction alone — improves fit or enables discovery. This is the central claim of the paper, and it is evidentially unestablished.

---

## Section-by-Section Notes

**Section 2.1 — Model**: The factorization Λ(**x**) = Θ ξ(**x**) follows Fox and Dunson (2015) directly; the extension to Poisson via Pólya-Gamma augmentation is the main technical novelty. The paper should be clearer about what is new here versus what is inherited. Currently the presentation gives equal billing to both.

**Section 2.2 — GL-GP**: The covariance formula for H̃ references three tuning parameters {ε, K, t}, but only eigenvalues μ_{i,p,c} and eigenvectors ṽ_{i,p,c} appear explicitly in the displayed equation. The role of ε (bandwidth) and t (diffusion time) is not shown in the formula itself; readers cannot reconstruct the kernel without Dunson et al. (2022). This is a reproducibility problem for the main method section.

**Section 3 — Simulations**: The comparison with GPWP (Nejatbakhsh et al., 2023) notes that GPWP is applied "on a single trial," while all latent factor models presumably use the full 100-observation training set. This is not explicitly clarified, and if true, it is an unfair comparison that flatters all latent factor models in the log-likelihood evaluation. The GPWP tuning by 5-fold CV and the L-GP bandwidth from MCMC are different tuning strategies of unequal difficulty; the claim that "latent factor models are easier to fit" partly reflects this asymmetry rather than an inherent advantage. Only 6 replications are reported — given sensitivity to hyperparameters acknowledged in Section 2.3, this is thin.

**Section 4.1 — LFP dataset**: Only 4 trials from a single session of a 39-session dataset are used, chosen without stated criteria ("we choose LFP recordings from the 13th session"). The scientific conclusions drawn (pupil area reflects neural activity tuning) are described by the authors themselves as insufficiently supported ("for a more concrete conclusion for formal analysis, we may need to include more data"). Including weak scientific conclusions in the main results section that the authors immediately walk back weakens the paper. Additionally, the scientific motivation is based on primate studies (Joshi and Gold, 2020; Wang et al., 2012) applied to a mouse dataset; this transfer is not justified.

**Section 4.2 — HC dataset**: The paper states "every neuron has the same input (time and directional position)," which is simply the structure of the dataset, not a limitation of dCMP. The argument that dCMP fails because it cannot share strength across neurons (leading to choice 1 or choice 2) is correct, but the chosen comparison design exploits this rather than correcting for it.

**Section 5 — Discussion**: The Discussion honestly enumerates the method's limitations (scalability, latent dimension selection, hyperparameter sensitivity). This is commendable but also reveals that the most important limitations are not addressed within the paper: scalability to genuinely large datasets, and the need for variational approximations. These are not future extensions — they are requirements for the "massive data" claim in the title.

**No MCMC convergence diagnostics**: Given three hyperparameters per GL-GP, sequential sampling of ξ(**x**) elements, and the known sensitivity of Pólya-Gamma augmentation for Poisson models, convergence assessment is non-trivial. No trace plots, Gelman-Rubin statistics, or effective sample sizes are reported in the main text.

---

## Strengths

- The combination of Pólya-Gamma augmentation for Poisson observations with the Fox-Dunson factor model is technically clean and correctly derived. The derivation of the pseudo-response conditional distribution in Section 2.3 is a genuine contribution.
- The simulation domain ("two boxes connecting with a tunnel") is a well-designed testbed for restricted-input GP, directly illustrating the failure mode of Euclidean kernels and giving intuitive content to the GL-GP advantage.
- The model structure reduces naturally to GPFA and LDS as special cases (Section 2.1), placing the contribution within the neuroscience literature clearly.
- Code is included in the supplementary material (MATLAB), which supports reproducibility.

---

## Missing Parts and Places to Improve

### Missing Experiments

1. **Mean-only vs. mean-covariance ablation**: Add a latent factor model that uses GP/GL-GP for the mean (ψ(**x**)) but holds Σ(**x**) fixed (e.g., Σ(**x**) = Θ Θ' + Σ₀ with Θ fixed). Without this, the covariance regression component is never tested in isolation. This is the most important missing experiment because it directly addresses the paper's core claim.

2. **Larger-scale simulation (n = 200–1000 neurons)**: To justify the "massive data" framing, test the method and report held-out likelihood and wall-clock time at scales relevant to modern probes. Even on a laptop, this would clarify where the method breaks and where the scalability limit lies.

3. **Fair dCMP comparison in HC data**: Fit a per-neuron Poisson-log-normal model (marginalizing out the latent factor) as a direct comparison against dCMP that controls for joint vs. separate modeling. This isolates whether the observation model or the joint structure drives the improvement.

### Deeper Analysis Needed

1. **Quantified benefit of restricted-input modeling**: In both the simulation and real data, the GL-GP improvement over standard GP is modest in the Gaussian case and more pronounced in the Poisson case. A systematic analysis of how the benefit scales with the degree of restriction in the input space (or the topology of the restricted domain) would clarify when GL-GP is actually necessary versus a Euclidean-kernel GP.

2. **Posterior uncertainty quantification**: Given the MCMC framework, credible intervals for fitted mean and covariance trajectories should be reported. Figure 2E-F and Figure 3C-D show point estimates with no uncertainty. For a Bayesian method, this omission is notable.

### Visualizations & Case Studies

1. **Direct visualization of where GL-GP differs from L-GP**: A difference plot (L-GLGP minus L-GP fitted covariance/mean) over the restricted domain would make the method's geometric advantage concrete. Currently the reader must compare two heatmaps and infer the improvement.

### Obvious Next Steps

1. **Multiple sessions / subjects in LFP analysis**: Using 4 trials from 1 session does not support the claim of a pupil–LFP relationship. An application to the full Steinmetz (2019) dataset (39 sessions) would constitute a genuine scientific finding and justify the method's use.

---

## Overall Assessment

The paper combines two existing frameworks — Fox and Dunson (2015)'s covariance regression and Dunson et al. (2022)'s graph Laplacian GP — and extends them to Poisson (spike count) data via Pólya-Gamma augmentation. The technical execution is sound, and the motivation from neuroscience is genuine. However, the paper has two structural problems that the experiments cannot resolve without fundamental redesign: (1) the "massive neural data" framing is unsupported by either the experimental scale (n = 14–50) or the computational approach (MCMC on laptop hardware), and (2) the primary comparison in real data conflates the proposed covariance regression component with the unrelated advantage of joint multi-neuron modeling, making the headline contribution unverifiable. A third problem—the absence of any comparison against a mean-only model—means the paper's core claim (that covariate-dependent covariance modeling is beneficial) rests on no direct evidence. These are not issues addressable by adding ablations; they reflect the choice of baselines and the scope of the experimental claims. The paper should not be accepted in its current form. A substantially revised version, with accurate scoping, corrected baselines, and direct ablations of the covariance component, would be a meaningful contribution to the neuroscience methods literature — though likely better suited to a statistics or neuroscience journal than ICLR.
### Strength Finder
## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)

1. **Unified framework for joint mean-covariance regression on both continuous and count data**: The paper extends the latent factor covariance regression to Poisson observations via a Poisson log-normal model (Section 2.1, equations for E(Y_j) and Cov(Y_j)), and introduces a Pólya-Gamma augmentation scheme for tractable MCMC inference (Section 2.3). This is a substantive extension over prior work like Fox & Dunson (2015), which only handled continuous responses, and is critical for neuroscience where spike count data are ubiquitous.

2. **Incorporation of graph structure for restricted covariates improves inference**: The GL-GP construction (Section 2.2) uses eigenpairs of the graph Laplacian to define a covariance kernel that respects intrinsic geometry of restricted input spaces. In the simulation study with the "two boxes with tunnel" domain, L-GLGP-adaptive consistently outperforms standard L-GP in held-out log-likelihood (Figure 1B,E; Section D.1), and in the LFP application, L-GLGP-adaptive achieves the highest held-out log-likelihood (Figure 2D), validating the benefit of encoding domain restrictions.

3. **Substantial improvement over existing baselines on real neural data**: On the hippocampus spike count dataset, L-GLGP-adaptive achieves a held-out log-likelihood of −5.89×10³, compared to −9.90×10³ for independent dCMP models and −6.24×10³ for L-GP (Section 4.2), demonstrating both the value of joint multi-neuron modeling and the benefit of graph-based covariance. This is a meaningful gap on real data.

### Supporting strengths

1. **Scalable factorization of covariate-dependent loadings**: The factorization Λ(x) = Θξ(x) (Section 2.1) reduces parameters from nkp to nL + Lkp, with L adaptively chosen via multiplicative shrinkage priors (Bhattacharya & Dunson, 2011). This makes the model feasible for modern high-dimensional neural recordings, as evidenced by applications with n=36 neurons (HC) and n=14 regions (LFP).

2. **Neuroscientifically interpretable results**: In the HC dataset, the first four PCs of the fitted mean correspond to known hippocampal neuron types—interneurons, location-selective, and direction-selective neurons (Section 4.2, Figure 3C)—and the model captures drifting place fields over time, a well-known phenomenon. In the LFP data, the model reveals that neural activity tends to concentrate at center locations when pupil area is small (Figure 2E,F), consistent with known LC-NE system modulation.

3. **Robustness to latent dimension in the Poisson case with GL-GP**: The L-GLGP-adaptive model shows similar held-out log-likelihoods for k=2 and k=5 in Poisson simulations, while L-GP is sensitive to this choice (Section 3, Figure 1E; Section D.1). This is a practically important property since selecting k is often difficult a priori.

### Presentation / practical strengths

1. **Efficient MCMC for count data**: The sequential sampling strategy for ξ(x) (Section 2.3) and the NB approximation to Poisson via PG augmentation keep per-iteration costs manageable—3.5 seconds per iteration for the LFP data (n=14, p=700) and 3.3 seconds for the HC data (n=36, p=480), both run on a laptop CPU (Section 4.1–4.2).

## Summary

The paper proposes a nonparametric mean-covariance regression framework for high-dimensional neural data that handles both continuous and count observations while accounting for restricted covariate domains via graph-Laplacian Gaussian processes. The most important evidence supporting this contribution is the consistent improvement in held-out log-likelihood of L-GLGP-adaptive over both standard GP-based latent factor models and existing baselines (GPWP, independent dCMP) across simulations (Figure 1B,E) and two real neural datasets (Figures 2D and Section 4.2), alongside interpretable neuroscientific findings in the applications.

