**Reasoning:** VT-WM is more ambitious than the 6.0-band tactile papers (SITR, NtQqIcSbqv) — it goes from representation to real-robot CEM planning — which argues for a score above 6. It is closer in scope to FLIP (6.5), which also uses a visual world model for manipulation planning. VT-WM adds tactile sensing and a more rigorous Section 4.1 evaluation, but has the underpowered planning evaluation and the missing ablation that FLIP-level papers did not have flagged against them. The paper falls short of the rigor that put Privileged Sensing at 8.5.

Placing this at **6.0**: a genuine, novel contribution to multi-modal world models for manipulation with solid imagination-quality evidence, held back from a higher score by an underpowered planning evaluation and a missing isolation ablation.

**Originality:** High — first multi-task VT-WM.  
**Importance:** High — contact-rich manipulation is a core open problem.  
**Claims vs. support:** Mixed — Section 4.1 well-supported; Section 4.2 underpowered; Section 4.3 framing inaccurate.  
**Experimental soundness:** Good for perception metrics, weak for planning.  
**Clarity:** Good overall.  
**Value to community:** Solid; opens a new research direction.

